    "case_id": "379",
    "case_summary": "A White man in his 60s presented with a 2-month history of diplopia and pain. Medical history was significant for recurring forehead lesion, first excised 6 years prior to presentation. Initial histopathologic findings showed a Breslow thickness of 6 mm, 14 mitotic figures/mm2, no ulceration, and extensive perineural and intraneural invasion. The lesion recurred twice: two years prior to presentation (excised with negative margins) and eight months prior to presentation (excised with clear margins, followed by 48-Gy brachytherapy to the glabella).\n\nVisual acuity was 20/20 bilaterally without afferent pupillary defect. Examination revealed left proptosis and resistance to globe retropulsion with edema, erythema, and ptosis of the left upper eyelid. Adduction was limited on the left, with associated strabismus and forehead anesthesia in the V1 division of the trigeminal nerve.\n\n- Imaging Studies:    1. MRI finding: Left superior orbit mass extending along the supraorbital nerve to the cavernous sinus, with indistinct borders and fat stranding       Image Title: T1-Weighted Coronal Magnetic Resonance Image of Orbit With Gadolinium Contrast       Image Description: Intense enhancement of the V1 branch of the left frontal nerve with associated fat stranding        - Pathological Examination:    1. Frontal nerve biopsy:       Image Title: Malignant Melanoma in a Nerve       Image Description: Cross section of nerve showing malignant cells with mitotic activity, effacing residual nerve axons, disrupted neuronal cells, and peritumoral and intratumoral lymphocytes",
    "frontier": [
